          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's love for Lear as a true emotional state. It answers Lear's explicit request for his daughters to state how much they love him; whether the avowal is sincere is separate from its clear truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The words avow a present state but do not themselves promise future conduct, renew an obligation, or undertake a specified commitment. The surrounding filial relationship and Lear's ensuing grant do not turn this exact expression into Goneril's promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression is a responsive declaration in a formally solicited speech, not a reflex-like or affective verbal discharge. The elaborate emotional rhetoric surrounding it does not by itself establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\\n\\nGONERIL.\\nSir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Its core force is adequately represented as an avowal of love under T. Although it also functions contextually as an answer in Lear's competitive distribution of bounty, the focal words do not exhibit a distinct core force inadequately captured by T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks which daughter loves him most while linking their answers to the distribution of his kingdom. Called on first, Goneril addresses Lear with the focal avowal as the opening of an extended, hyperbolic account of her love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no identifiable undertaking by Goneril. Lear accepts her answer sufficiently to grant her and Albany territory, but this is uptake of her answer in the love test, not acceptance of a promise contained in “I love you.” The supplied scene does not settle whether her professed affection is genuine.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognised as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare’s King Lear.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence establishes the utterance's avowal force and immediate setting, but it does not by itself establish Goneril's sincerity or actual emotional state. No omitted context is treated as absent from the work.\"}"
        }
      ],
